            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Overall, the air leakage from your home is quite high, and there are some key locations where this exists.



- The damper for the main floor fireplace was wide open when I arrived and you had mentioned that it may have been that way for quite some time. We did close it for the blower door test, and that fireplace is still relatively leaky, so looking at a high efficiency wood stove insert (that is properly sealed &amp; has a dedicated outside combustion air connected) is a good option for you.



- Some of the older windows did not seal well, allowing some air leakage &amp; heat loss. It would cost alot of money to upgrade these windows and although there is a longer term payback (see the windows section of this report), I think it is more effective right now to make sure that all windows are fully closed &amp; latched during the winter heating season. For example, we had found the newer window in the lower level bedroom (near the cold storage room) was partially open, causing that room to be extra cold ........ and drawing heat out from the rest of the house.



- There appeared to be some colder air coming up through the heating system floor vents on the main floor - including the bedroom on the lower level that you use for the B&amp;B. You should conduct a "Garbage Bag" air leakage test (I will email you the procedure) to check the airflow through to the various floor vents on the main level. If you find one or 2 that are not delivering a good volume of air ...... you may find that the ductwork supplying that heat vent is not well sealed or has come apart ? 



- The front door requires an update to the weather-stripping.



- The attic hatch requires weather-stripping or could be sealed with a removable caulking (eg: Draft Attack).



- The 2 skylights in the second floor kitchen are both problem issues, allowing air leakage into the attic &amp; heat loss to the outside because they are really only about R2, where your attic insulation is currently about 7" (R28). In my opinion, these 2 skylights are not worth replacing with new Energy Star models - it would be better to remove them, seal the roof &amp; ceiling, enhance the attic insulation where they are removed, and install one really nice LED light fixture in the centre of your kitchen.



- Both bathroom exhaust fans appear to allow heat loss even when the fans are not operating. You may want to consider upgrading them to a new Energy Star models that have good butterfly dampers, uses less electricity &amp; operates a lot quieter.



- Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls. For these electrical outlets, you can install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates. There were a few holes into the exterior walls for cables &amp; wires &amp; these should be sealed.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The software predicts approximately a 10% reduction is easily achieved in your house, where you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 378&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. Personally, I think you can achieve a minimum 20% reduction, doubling the estimated energy savings &amp; increasing the rebate to $600.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <windows>

The windows throughout your house are a mixture of older &amp; newer wood &amp; vinyl windows - some are double pane, some are "narrow" triple pane, and there are a mixture of picture windows, sliders &amp; a few casement units. My "Low E" coating detector did not recognize any special coatings on any of the windows - older or newer. The exterior walls of 100% of the house have been previously retrofitted with an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene rigid foam insulation and a new hardy board siding. This would have been the best time to update older windows, however I fully understand that the cost of new windows is quite significant.



For your curiosity and longer term energy efficiency planning, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the current windows to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of the current windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,334&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;34.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. As discussed, the payback on windows (from strictly an energy savings perspective) is quite long, however most homeowners consider upgrading for other reasons - current windows have broken hardware or frames, the seals between the glass have deteriorated, wanting to switch from sliders to casement, etc. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank, and the cost of electricity in Norman Wells is very expensive. In fact, electricity in Norman Wells is generated primarily using diesel and the efficiency of the diesel generators is often less than if you used the oil directly to create the hot water.



The Arctic Energy Alliance is starting to see a variety of higher efficiency oil hot water heaters being installed in all regions of the Northwest Territories (most are direct vent through a side wall, rather than up a chimney). Although we do not recommend or endorse any one manufacturer or product, I will mention a unit that we are seeing more NWT contractors starting to install - the Toyotomi OM-122DW (and I saw some in Norman Wells). We are advised by Natural Resources Canada that this unit is not currently Canadian ENERGY STAR® rated, however they indicate it has an Energy Factor of 0.682.



The AEA adjudicates the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP) and the current guidelines indicate that an instantaneous on-demand oil hot water heater must have a minimum 0.68 EF and be Canadian ENERGY STAR® rated. As such, the Toyotomi OM-122DW is not eligible for the $1,250 EEIP rebate ........... however we are currently reviewing this with the GNWT - so please call me or check our website for an update. 





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot electric hot water tank with an instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a 0.682 EF, there would be a small increase in overall energy use, however the cost difference between oil &amp; electricity indicates that you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save up to $ 846&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. As mentioned above, this upgrade does not currently qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current house currently has 2 newer high efficiency furnaces that are direct vent units (ie: they draw their combustion air from outside and then vent combustion products directly out through a side wall chimney vent). I have modeled these 2 furnaces at the lower-end default 86% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) that is listed on the Manufacturer Brochure and what is listed in the NRCan database. As outlined in my email to you, I noticed in my pictures that there is a "combustion test" results printout taped to the front of each burner ............. and I would really appreciate it if you could take closer pictures of these &amp; email them to me. 



There is also a Blaze King wood stove on the upper floor that draws its' combustion air from inside the house. And there is a wood fireplace on the main floor that also draws its' combustion air from inside the house.



Both the wood stove and the wood fireplace are using inside "heated air" to burn &amp; send up the chimney, which draws down the overall efficiency (amount of net heat delivered inside the house) of these 2 wood burning appliances. The wood stove door is normally in the closed position which helps to reduce the amount of inside air that will continuously flow up through the stove &amp; out the chimney. We found that the wood fireplace chimney damper was in the wide open position, so this fireplace had been continuously drawing inside warmer air up &amp; out of your house .......... making the main floor feel much colder.



You had mentioned that you want to install a high efficiency wood stove insert on the main floor, to replace the current fireplace. If you proceed with this, I would suggest that you have the same installation contractor install a dedicated outside combustion air pipe (sealed to the back or bottom of the wood stove) so that both the new wood stove and your current Blaze King are not drawing any inside air for combustion.



For now, I just want to estimate the energy savings associated with installing these 2 outside combustion air pipes. For further estimates about energy savings by heating with wood - please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing 2 outside combustion air pipes (one for the current Blaze King and the second for the new wood stove you are planning to install on the main floor), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 182&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There is a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. rebate for eligible wood stoves installation - and this is outlined further in the "Additional Comments" section of this report. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front Southwest facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 785&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year in a thermal electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets that are older 13 litre model units, and you should consider replacing them with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing toilets with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



This house has multiple components comprising the ventilation design:



1. A kitchen range hood fan that does not vent to the outside (it is an internal recirculation model).



2. There are 2 older bathroom exhaust fans, operated by a regular switch. An easy upgrade is to install a timer switch (5 -10-15-20 minutes) so the fan will be operated long enough to exhaust odours &amp; humidity, however cannot be accidentally left on all day, wasting large amounts of heated air from your house. These bathroom exhaust fans should be upgraded to new Energy Star models that have good butterfly dampers, uses less electricity &amp; operates alot quieter.



3. The clothes dryer is vented to the outside. Please note the dryer did not seem to have a good airflow to the outside, so please check the exhaust pipe on a regular basis for a buildup of clothes lint or a kink in the pipe.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Wood Stove Heating&lt;/b&gt;



During my visit, we had talked about your interest to install a wood stove insert where the fireplace is currently located on the main floor of the house. If you choose to proceed with this upgrade, the unit should have a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply (the same as what I would like you to have installed for the Blaze King wood stove on the upper floor.



You are currently using the Blaze King for heating the upper floor, however the far bedrooms on the 2nd floor and much of the main floor feels colder as the wood heat does not circulate well. Your house has 2 newer high efficiency Granby oil furnaces that are "direct vent" models and have the high efficiency ECM blower motors to circulate heat. Many people will use the high efficiency blower motor to also circulate wood heat around the house, however the current setup in your house is not great for that.



The one furnace is a "downdraft" model and heats the main floor. The other furnace is an "updraft" model and heats the 2nd floor. The return air for both furnaces is on the lower floor - one inside the lower floor bathroom, and the other on the same wall but just outside that bathroom. There is a central open concept stairwell between the main &amp; 2nd floors. In one way, having the return air at floor level of the main floor is a good thing for heating ....... as you are always drawing the coldest air from the lowest level of the house. However, for circulating heat from wood stoves ...... you want to have an alternate high wall return air.



Some homes have both lower &amp; upper wall return air pipes, where you can open the lower &amp; close the upper when you are heating with the oil furnace. When using the furnace to circulate warm air from the wood stove, the lower return is closed and the upper return air is opened. You may want to check with your heating contractor to see if something like this is possible for your house, and how they would set up the furnace so you could select a "fan circulation" only setting.



For now ................. I did a quick estimate of the energy savings if you were able to use the Blaze King to offset 50% of the heating demand from your house - projecting about $1,882 of estimated annual savings (these savings are net of oil saved &amp; $100 per cord for self-harvested wood). Based on the current setup of the furnaces &amp; the wood heat - I think this estimate is undervalued and you folks indeed know how much oil you are truly able to offset !!!



&lt;b&gt;Rebates:&lt;/b&gt;



If you were to install an eligible wood stove insert on the lower main floor, you would qualify for &lt;b&gt;1/3 of the purchase cost up to $ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (2 bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.5 Pascal. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment would not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. The furnaces are direct vent models, however the Blaze King wood stove on the 2nd floor currently draws its' combustion air from inside the house.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be a functioning smoke detector, however a carbon monoxide (CO) detector could not be located. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date (normally every 10 years). Some of your current smoke detectors appeared to be older &amp; perhaps in need of replacement.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Norman Wells&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.52 per litre 

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.14 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



